    "long_instructions": "## Webpage Responsiveness\r\n\r\nImprove how a webpage works across different screen sizes.\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Test\r\n- Resize the browser window and switch between **Desktop** and **Mobile** views.\r\n- Identify specific issues using the **Responsiveness Checklist**.\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Plan Your Prompt\r\n- Choose **one issue** to fix.\r\n- In `prompt.md` plan your goal, constraints, and any edge cases to avoid.\r\n- Add `prompt.md` as context in AI.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Fix\r\n- Use your plan to write your AI prompt.\r\n- Ask AI to fix the issue.\r\n- Describe the changes made and save the new version.\r\n\r\n### Step 4: Improve\r\n- Test your changes across screen sizes.\r\n- If you have time, fix **one more issue**.\r\n\r\n:::details [Need help prompting AI?]\r\n- “On small screens, make the layout stack into a single column while keeping the text readable.”\r\n- “Make the navigation fit on mobile without running off the screen.”\r\n- “Ensure images scale to fit smaller screens without overflowing.”\r\n:::",
    "teacher_markdown": "### Teaching Tips\r\n\r\nEncourage students to act like test engineers by using the Responsiveness Checklist in their Unit Guide.\r\n\r\n**The Resize Detective:**  \r\nHave students resize their browser and ask: “What changes? What breaks?” Students should name issues using the checklist before prompting AI.\r\n\r\n**The One-Issue Rule:**  \r\nIf students try to fix everything, redirect them to one specific issue (e.g., navigation, text, images).\r\n\r\n**The Small Fix Mindset:**  \r\nIf students are stuck, prompt them to focus on small, clear fixes (e.g., stacking layout, resizing images, wrapping text), not a full redesign.",
    "display_name": "ExploreWorld",
    "bubble_choice_description": "A travel website looks great on desktop but breaks on smaller screens. Find and fix responsiveness issues so it works across devices.",
